Tabulated EAM potentials
========================

critic2's EAM backend reads embedded-atom potentials in the LAMMPS/DYNAMO
"setfl" (.eam.alloy) and "eam/fs" (.eam.fs) formats -- the same files LAMMPS
uses with pair_style eam/alloy and eam/fs.

A potential can always be named explicitly, and then nothing here is consulted:

    EDIT RELAX EAM /path/to/Cu_mishin1.eam.alloy

Otherwise critic2 picks one from this directory. The candidates are listed in
"index", one per line:

    <file name>   <element symbols it covers> ...

The FIRST entry whose elements cover every species in the system wins, so the
order of the index is the selection policy: the community-standard potential
for each element first, then the Zhou-2004 universal set, then alloy-specific
potentials, then a 16-metal Zhou file as a last resort for arbitrary mixtures.
Add your own by copying the file here and adding a line. The element symbols
must match the file's own header (line 4); they let critic2 decide whether a
potential covers a structure without opening it.

Only setfl and eam/fs are read. Formats that begin like setfl but carry extra
tables -- .adp (angular-dependent), .cdeam (concentration-dependent) -- and
the single-element DYNAMO funcfl .eam layout are rejected with an error.

Coverage
--------

Metals: Ag Al Au Co Cr Cu Fe Mg Mo Ni Pb Pd Pt Ta Ti V W Zr (18), plus H
through NiAlH_jea. Every binary among the 16 Zhou metals is available through
Zhou2004_16metals.eam.alloy, and there are specifically-fitted potentials for
Al-Cu, Al-Fe, Co-Al, Cu-Ni, Cu-Ta, Cu-Zr, V-Fe and Ni-Al-H.

Not covered: the alkali and alkaline-earth metals (Li Na K Rb Cs, Be Ca Sr Ba),
Sc Mn Zn Y Nb Tc Ru Rh Cd Hf Re Os Ir Hg, the post-transition metals Ga In Sn
Tl Bi, and all lanthanides and actinides. EAM potentials exist for several of
these elsewhere; see the note on sources below.

Provenance and licence
----------------------

Two sources, both compatible with critic2's GPL-3-or-later:

1. Debian's lammps-data package (the LAMMPS potentials/ directory,
   https://www.lammps.org). Debian's copyright file places these, with the
   rest of LAMMPS, under the GNU GPL "either version 2 of the License, or (at
   your option) any later version" -- i.e. GPL-2-or-later. See
   /usr/share/doc/lammps-data/copyright for the full text and contributors.
   These are: Cu_mishin1, Fe_mm, Mg_mm, Zr_mm, AlCu, CuNi, CoAl, CuTa,
   AlFe_mm, CuZr_mm, VFe_mm and NiAlH_jea.

2. Generated here from the parameters of Zhou, Johnson and Wadley, Phys. Rev.
   B 69, 144113 (2004) -- and, for chromium, Lin, Johnson and Zhigilei, Phys.
   Rev. B 77, 214108 (2008) -- using the generator LAMMPS distributes in
   tools/eam_database (create_eam.py + eamDatabase.py + the EAM_code parameter
   database, also GPL-2-or-later):

       python3 create_eam.py -n Cu                          # one element
       python3 create_eam.py -n Cu Ag Au Ni Pd Pt Al Pb \
                                Fe Mo Ta W Mg Co Ti Zr      # the 16-metal file

   The generated Cu-Ta file reproduces the Debian CuTa.eam.alloy to 4e-6
   relative over all 14006 tabulated values (that file was written in lower
   precision), which is what validates this route. Note the Fortran generator
   create.f in the same directory cannot be used for more than eight elements:
   its element line is a fixed 24-character field, so it silently truncates.

Caveats
-------

* The Zhou-2004 parameters are fitted to the PURE metals; alloy interactions
  in Zhou2004_16metals.eam.alloy come from mixing rules and are, in the
  generator's own words, "inferior to models where the mixed terms were
  specifically optimized for particular alloys". Prefer the alloy-specific
  files above it in the index, and check any mixed system against published
  results.
* VFe_mm.eam.fs and CuZr_mm.eam.fs carry no citation in their headers. They
  are kept because VFe_mm is the only source here for vanadium and CuZr_mm
  covers the Cu-Zr metallic-glass system.
* Inclusion is not an endorsement.

